      "Shipped a dual-backend idempotency marker: Lead.bufferPostedVariantKeys (string[], Convex schema.ts + convex/leads.ts's leadReturn/mapLeadRow/new updateBufferPostedVariantKeys mutation; SQLite bufferPostedVariantKeys TEXT column + serializeLeadBufferPostedVariantKeys/parseLeadBufferPostedVariantKeys in sqlite-row-mapping.ts; db.ts's new updateLeadBufferPostedVariantKeys, mirroring updateLeadBufferProfileIds's dual-backend shape) recording which pack variants have already been queued to which Buffer profile. The key (bufferPostedVariantKey, src/lib/marketing/buffer.ts) is `${pack.generatedAt}|${profileId}|${contentType}|${index}` — deliberately scoped to the pack's own generatedAt rather than yearMonth, so a mid-month regenerate (same month, new content) makes every prior key stale and the new content postable again, instead of permanently blocking it. scheduleMarketingPackToBufferAction now builds an alreadyPostedKeys set (pruneBufferPostedVariantKeysForPack, filtering out any key from a different generatedAt so the persisted list can't grow forever across a lead's lifetime) before its per-profile loop, skips any variant whose key is already present (labeled '...: already posted this month' in the skipped array, so a re-click is visibly a no-op rather than silently doing nothing), and after the loop persists the pruned-plus-newly-posted key set — but only when at least one new key was actually posted, and only via a best-effort write (no requireConvex fail-closed): the Buffer posts that already succeeded cannot be undone, so a marker-persistence failure must not turn a successful schedule into a reported error — worst case on a Convex outage, the very next click can re-post the same variants once more, no worse than the behavior before this guard shipped. This deliberately does NOT reuse LEAD_BUFFER_PROFILES_CONVEX_PRIMARY's fail-closed contract, since that gates an operator-initiated toggle-and-save (safe to fail loudly and let the operator retry) while this gates automatic bookkeeping after an already-irreversible side effect. Known, accepted limitation (documented, not engineered around): two browser tabs clicking 'Send pack to Buffer' for the same lead at the same instant could both read the same pre-write alreadyPostedKeys and both post the same variant once before either write lands — a low-probability, single-operator-UI race, not the money-critical webhook-redelivery class AGENT_PROTOCOL's idempotency rules target, and out of proportion to fix with a Convex-side atomic-merge mutation for this surface. New test coverage in buffer-actions.test.ts (5 cases): marker persisted with the correct keys after a successful schedule; an already-posted variant is skipped (not re-posted) while a genuinely-new sibling variant for the same profile still schedules; no write at all when every variant was already posted; a stale key from a prior pack generation is pruned rather than carried forward, and the new pack's identically-indexed variant schedules normally; the schedule still reports success when the marker-persistence call itself rejects. Verified: npx tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.json (0 errors) + npm run lint (clean) + npm test (2657 passed, up from 2652 — 5 new, 198 files unchanged) + a full clean rebuild (`rm -rf .next && npm run build`, clean, same 42-route ○/ƒ split) + npm run dev sanity check (/, /dashboard/leads, /docs/marketing-automation, /preview/example-bakery all 200; dev server killed, local.db deleted afterward). NEXT_STEPS unchanged — all five gated items (stripe-price-objects, lead-ingestion, marketing-content-scheduling, commerce-payouts-stripe-connect, github-actions-runner-unavailable) remain operator/legal/infra-blocked; this closes a concretely-deferred follow-up from the immediately-preceding iteration, not any gated item. Diff-gate anchor stays at dda421a (targeted, single-item follow-up, not a full tier 1-5 sweep).",
